The two models, verified:

```js
// CONTROLLED — React state is the truth, DOM follows
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
<input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
// every keystroke: onChange → setState → re-render → input displays state
// the input CAN'T show anything state doesn't have (can't desync)

// UNCONTROLLED — DOM holds the truth, React reads on demand
const inputRef = useRef();
<input defaultValue="" ref={inputRef} />
const value = inputRef.current.value;  // read when needed (submit)
// typing doesn't re-render; the DOM just holds its own value
```

The trade-off table (the interview core):
- Source of truth: React state vs the DOM
- Re-renders: every keystroke vs none until read
- Instant validation per-keystroke: trivial (state always current) vs manual (listen to input events)
- Conditional formatting (input masks, capitalization): natural in controlled vs fights the DOM
- File inputs: CANNOT be controlled (browser security) — always uncontrolled
- Simple forms, huge forms: uncontrolled avoids re-render churn; libraries like react-hook-form use it
- Wiring cost: controlled needs value+onChange per input; uncontrolled needs refs + submit handler

The hybrid (the real-world answer): use controlled for inputs needing live reaction (validation, dependent fields, search-as-you-type) and uncontrolled for large forms where re-render cost matters — or react-hook-form, which keeps inputs uncontrolled (registering refs) and only pulls state at validation/submit, combining controlled ergonomics with uncontrolled performance.
